※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=42 diag=none

※ A tuple written after an asterism is handed over as several arguments rather
※ than as one.  The glyph is three asterisks arranged as a single mark, which is
※ what it says: several things stand where one is written.
※
※ What the members become are ordinary arguments, so they are counted and their
※ types are checked exactly as if they had been written out one at a time, and
※ ordinary arguments may stand before them and after them.  A call may spread
※ more than one tuple, since each simply contributes its members in order, and a
※ tuple whose members are of different types spreads into parameters of those
※ types.
※
※ Nothing is read from memory for any of this.  A tuple is its members held
※ separately, so spreading one is a matter of which registers the call is handed,
※ not of taking anything apart.

fn three(a: u6, b: u6, c: u6) → u6:
    a + b + c

fn four(a: u6, b: u16, c: u6, d: u16) → u6:
    if b = 4u16 ∧ d = 4u16:
        a + c
    else:
        0u6

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let pair: 〈u6, u6〉 = 〈1u6, 2u6〉
    ※ the members of the tuple, then an argument written out
    let head: u6 = three(⁂pair, 4u6)
    ※ an argument written out, then the members
    let tail: u6 = three(4u6, ⁂pair)
    ※ one tuple's members and then another's, each landing on its own type
    let mixed: 〈u6, u16〉 = 〈14u6, 4u16〉
    let both: u6 = four(⁂mixed, ⁂mixed)
    ※ 7 + 7 + 28 = 42
    head + tail + both
